    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   },
   {
    "quote": ". In the classic experiment, participants spun a wheel of fortune that landed on a number between 0 and 100, then estimated the percentage of African nations in the United Nations. The wheel was rigged. Participants who saw 10 gave a median estimate of 25%; those who saw 65 gave a median estimate of 45% (",
    "url": "https://www.koji.so/docs/anchoring-bias-research",
    "pass": 3,
    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   },
   {
    "quote": "The number was visibly random and had nothing to do with Africa or the UN — yet it pulled estimates toward it. We anchor on whatever number is in front of us and adjust insufficiently from there.",
    "url": "https://www.koji.so/docs/anchoring-bias-research",
    "pass": 3,
    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   },
   {
    "quote": "Asking \"How many hours per week do you spend on this — more or less than 20?\" anchors the estimate near 20.",
    "url": "https://www.koji.so/docs/anchoring-bias-research",
    "pass": 3,
    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   },
   {
    "quote": "Drop \"more or less than X\" framings; ask for the estimate cold.",
    "url": "https://www.koji.so/docs/anchoring-bias-research",
    "pass": 3,
    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   },
   {
    "quote": "reference point that pulls quantitative estimates toward it.",
    "url": "https://www.koji.so/docs/anchoring-bias-research",
    "pass": 3,
    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   },
   {
    "quote": "Order bias is about how earlier questions reshape the context for later ones; anchoring is about a specific value becoming the starting point a respondent adjusts from. A price shown in question two can anchor an estimate in question five.",
    "url": "https://www.koji.so/docs/anchoring-bias-research",
    "pass": 3,
    "first_seen": "2026-11-06"
   }
